WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.450
- Congratulations graduands.

00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:06.240
- To my fellow graduates, congratulations.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.840
- [Narrator] This year's college graduates

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:12.260
weather the pandemic storm
and their reward, jobs.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:14.970
Job openings in the US
topped 8 million in March

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:17.750
for the first time ever,
according to the labor department.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:19.610
- In every single industry we look at

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:22.490
hiring was up 69% year over year.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:25.470
This is an incredible time
to be looking for a job.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.610
Hiring is at pre-pandemic levels now.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:29.700
- [Narrator] The catch, getting those jobs

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.180
is extremely competitive.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.410
- College graduates last
year or younger workers

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:35.290
who are 20 to 24 years old

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:39.710
were terminated at a rate 79%
higher than older workers.

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:41.420
And so new grads who are
entering the workforce

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:42.580
are gonna face competition

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.400
from this younger group of workers

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.970
who bore the brunt of the recession.

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:48.840
- [Narrator] For some graduating
students hunting for jobs,

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:50.930
the competition can be overwhelming.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:52.670
- You might not believe me but I would say

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:55.020
I submitted close to 500 applications.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:58.190
- My roommates and I have
collectively applied to,

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:00.130
I mean like thousands at this point,

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:01.550
because it almost gets to the point

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:05.010
where you get a little,
not I won't say desperate,

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:06.200
but you get worried.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.690
- [Narrator] At the same
time, many companies say,

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:11.830
they are struggling to find
qualified workers to hire.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:14.260
And as some companies
retool their workforce,

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.850
there's an increased
demand for younger workers.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:18.670
As hiring continues to surge,

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:22.540
how should the class of 2021
approach the job market?

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.020
- There is a lot of hiring going on

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.930
and there are companies who are very eager

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:27.970
to get the best employees and to invest

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:29.090
in this kind of talent.

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:32.010
- [Narrator] Daniel Roth is the
editor in chief of LinkedIn.

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:33.950
The professional social media network

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.530
with over 750 million users.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.120
- The industries where
we're seeing the most growth

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:41.850
are any ones that have to do
with either moving you around

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:45.700
or keeping you healthy or
helping you be able to do things

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.340
no matter where you're located.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:50.680
- [Narrator] According to
LinkedIn, the top industry

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.660
with the most entry-level openings

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:55.240
is transportation and logistics.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:58.600
Followed by healthcare,
software and IT services,

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.690
retail and consumer goods.

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:03.720
Other industries weren't so lucky.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:06.860
According to Gusto, a
payroll and benefits company,

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:10.150
tourism, accommodations,
and arts and entertainment

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:12.860
saw the lowest growth
compared to March 2020.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:16.180
Luke Pardue, an economist with Gusto says,

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:18.880
getting that first job for a recent grad

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:20.970
can have a big impact on their future.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:23.030
- There's a whole body
of economic research

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:26.060
that demonstrates the
first job that you take

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:28.110
right out of college has
important implications

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:29.770
and can determine the trajectory

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:32.230
of your earnings and your career path.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:35.160
So finding a job that is
booked the right fit for you,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.580
and can allow you to grow your skills,

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.840
will have a lasting impact later in life,

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:42.040
and later in your career.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:43.720
- [Narrator] Natalie
Pawlowski is still trying

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:45.550
to land that first job.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:48.380
She's graduating in may
from Cornell University

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:50.020
and has been interviewing for jobs

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.950
in the financial services
industry for months.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.990
- It's very exhausting to have to continue

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:57.400
starting the process again,

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.410
and preparing for these interviews,

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:02.610
and that stress that comes
with taking these interviews

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:05.170
and speaking to people at these companies

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:08.540
and getting so far to
find out that, you know,

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.190
you came so close, however
you didn't make it.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.350
And you have to start right back again.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:14.650
- [Narrator] The good thing for Natalie

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:16.080
and students like her,

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.880
is that it seems like she's
looking in the right place.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.230
According to Handshake,

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.460
a job search site for college students,

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:25.310
financial services is
the number one industry

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:27.560
hiring the class of 2021.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:29.360
- So just in the past month of April,

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:34.360
we were up 209% for 2021
postings as compared to 2020.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.500
And when you go back and
even compare it to 2019,

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:41.850
we're still up about 48%.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:43.830
So things are certainly coming back,

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:46.050
and that is very promising
for our students.

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:48.180
- [Narrator] Christine
Cruzvergara, an executive

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.970
at Handshake, says students
need to be flexible

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:53.270
in the ways they think
about their careers.

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:54.770
- So they may have come into school,

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:56.750
thinking they would go
into certain industries

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:58.380
or a certain job function,

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.400
and the pandemic has sort of shown them

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.180
that either that industry
may not be so hot right now,

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:05.020
or they might wanna think
about a different way

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:06.560
to apply their skill.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:08.190
- [Narrator] Melissa Sarmiento-Alvarez

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:09.770
took that advice to heart.

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:12.940
She studied social relations
and policy at Michigan State.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:15.000
After completing a remote internship

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.940
at the Consumer Financial
Protection Bureau last summer,

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:20.910
she broadened her search
beyond government roles.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:22.510
- I interviewed with a few companies

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:24.500
that I was the only policy student

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.660
in a whole group of business students

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:29.660
and they almost liked
that, they were like,

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:32.667
you know what, not like
in a rude way, but like

00:04:32.667 --> 00:04:33.837
"what are you doing here?

00:04:33.837 --> 00:04:36.377
"Like why are you interested
in a sales position

00:04:36.377 --> 00:04:38.960
"if you studied social policy?"

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.500
And it's because I felt that
if I entered a different field,

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.870
a different realm, that
maybe I would stand out more.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.450
So that's what I did
and I think it helped me

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:50.450
later on in my search.

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:52.220
- [Narrator] Both handshake and LinkedIn

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:54.630
have additional advice for recent grads.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:56.430
Lean on your networks.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:05:00.120
- It is a tough job market,
and while jobs are rebounding,

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:03.490
I know the competition is
hard, but it is doable.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:06.540
And the real thing that you can focus on

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:08.640
is controlling your sphere
of influence through

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:10.850
- We know through our data
you are four times more likely

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:13.080
to get a job through your network

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:17.330
than through any other
method, so, exploit that.

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:18.410
You gotta make connections.

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:19.540
You have to go and find people.

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:20.980
You have to email people.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:22.760
- [Narrator] Melissa recently landed a job

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:24.500
as an investment specialist trainee

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:26.430
at Bank of America in Florida.

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:28.310
Eventually, she plans to move,

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:30.350
but for now, like so many jobs,

00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:32.140
it will be completely remote.

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:35.130
- Just having some sort
of security is a relief.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:36.070
It's such a huge relief.

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:37.660
And like having a company
that's like willing

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:39.577
to take you on board and say like,

00:05:39.577 --> 00:05:42.017
"Okay, like let's see what you can offer,

00:05:42.017 --> 00:05:43.470
"like, let's see what you
can bring to the table,"

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:45.094
like that's really exciting too.

00:05:45.094 --> 00:05:47.677
(gentle music)

